      "writtenOn": "2026-06-29T07:29:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think it\u0027s risky for a public function to not validate that the string actually starts with a `//` prefix. For example, it currently accepts an empty package: `parse_absolute(\":foo\")` which splits into `[\"\", \"foo\"]` and returns `Ok` with `package \u003d\u003d \"\"`, probably unintended?\n\nA later `package.as_str_without_slashes()` then does `\"\"[2..]` and panics. `parse()` guards this today, but `parse_absolute` is `pub`. Maybe we could restrict visibility here, or assert/return an error when `package` doesn\u0027t start with `//`?",
      "range": {
