      "writtenOn": "2026-06-29T07:29:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That `if pkg.as_str().len() \u003e 2` I guess is a check for \"is this (not) the root package `//`\", which I find a bit hard to understand for readers not used to the code and conventions.\n\n`as_str_without_slashes()` already returns `\"\"` for the root package `//` and `\"foo/bar\"` otherwise, could we use that property?\n\nWhat about extracting a helper:\n\n```rust\n/// Builds the output-relative path string for a file in `pkg`.\nfn relative_path(\u0026self, pkg: \u0026PackageRef, s: \u0026str) -\u003e String {\n  let pkg_dir \u003d pkg.as_str_without_slashes();\n  // The root package (\"//\") has no subdirectory, so no separator before `s`.\n  let sep \u003d if pkg_dir.is_empty() { \"\" } else { \"/\" };\n  format!(\"{}{pkg_dir}{sep}{s}\", self.root_source_dir_rel)\n}\n```\n\nThat should result in:\n- For the root package: `root_rel + \"\" + \"\" + s`.\n- For `//foo/bar`: `root_rel + \"foo/bar\" + \"/\" + s`.\n\nOr if you prefer it inlined, maybe this?\n\n```rust\nlet pkg_dir \u003d pkg.as_str_without_slashes();\nlet rel_path \u003d if pkg_dir.is_empty() {\n  format!(\"{}{s}\", self.root_source_dir_rel)\n} else {\n  format!(\"{}{pkg_dir}/{s}\", self.root_source_dir_rel)\n};\nOk(File::from_rust(rel_path))\n```",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 46,
        "startChar": 27,
        "endLine": 46,
        "endChar": 52
      },
